text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Creates an array where each element is produced by calling `f` with"},{"DocComment":" that element's index while walking forward through the array."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is essentially the same as writing"},{"DocComment":" ```text"},{"DocComment":" [f(0), f(1), f(2), …, f(N - 2), f(N - 1)]"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" and is similar to `(0..i).map(f)`, just for arrays not iterators."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If `N == 0`, this produces an empty array without ever calling `f`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Example"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" // type inference is helping us here, the way `from_fn` knows how many"},{"DocComment":" // elements to produce is the length of array down there: only arrays of"},{"DocComment":" // equal lengths can be compared, so the const generic parameter `N` is"},{"DocComment":" // inferred to be 5, thus creating array of 5 elements."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let array = core::array::from_fn(|i| i);"},{"DocComment":" // indexes are:    0  1  2  3  4"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(array, [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let array2: [usize; 8] = core::array::from_fn(|i| i * 2);"},{"DocComment":" // indexes are:     0  1  2  3  4  5   6   7"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(array2, [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14]);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let bool_arr = core::array::from_fn::<_, 5, _>(|i| i % 2 == 0);"},{"DocComment":" // indexes are:       0     1      2     3      4"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(bool_arr, [true, false, true, false, true]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You can also capture things, for example to create an array full of clones"},{"DocComment":" where you can't just use `[item; N]` because it's not `Copy`:"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let my_string: [String; 2] = std::array::from_fn(|i| format!(\"Hello {i}\"));"},{"DocCommen